          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Scarlet primarily signifies the deep stain of sin and guilt, while blood, atonement, deliverance, luxury, and corrupt splendor are established context-dependent alternatives.\",\"book_summary\":\"Scarlet signifies an outward display of covenant blood, including counterfeit displays by those possessing religious form without inward reality.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The stain or guilt of sin.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The outward sign of covenant blood, whether genuine or counterfeit.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book elevates the consensus's blood-and-atonement alternative into the governing meaning and reclassifies negative scarlet imagery, especially Revelation 17, as counterfeit covenant display rather than primarily sin, luxury, or corrupt power. It also treats Isaiah's scarlet sin imagery under a blood-display framework rather than as the color's central identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The principal referent changes from human sin or guilt to covenant blood manifested outwardly. Although both associations occur in the consensus, identifying scarlet itself with covenant blood replaces rather than merely narrows the consensus's dominant identification.\",\"rationale\":\"The genuine covenant-blood element is recognizable within the consensus alternatives, particularly the readings of Rahab's cord and scarlet wool. However, the book makes that secondary association the core and adds a counterfeit-form category for hostile or corrupt uses. Because sin and covenant blood are distinct referents—one the condition requiring cleansing, the other the asserted covenantal sign or means—the relationship is divergent despite substantial overlap in cited cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Joshua 2:18 and Hebrews 9:19 support an association between scarlet objects and deliverance or blood ritual, but neither explicitly defines scarlet as covenant blood; Revelation 17:4 does not identify the woman's clothing as a counterfeit covenant display, and Isaiah 1:18 directly likens scarlet to sins. Thus the unifying genuine-versus-counterfeit formulation is substantially inferential.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
